    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm running an organic kale operation up in the PNW and I'm running into some nitrogen issues. My soil pH is sitting around 6.2 right now and I've been hitting it with compost, but the leaves are still looking pretty pale. I'm looking to supplement the nitrogen directly so I don't mess with the pH too much. Between bone meal and blood meal, which one would you recommend for a quick nitrogen boost without spiking the pH?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Stick with bone meal for your needs, as it supplies a slow-release calcium, phosphorus, and magnesium that supports root development without significantly altering soil pH. Blood meal is an excellent source of immediate nitrogen, but its high calcium content and saline finish can cause temporary pH spikes that damage kale's already sensitive pH balance. Given your current 6.2 pH and natural fertilizer usage, bone meal may be your safer, more sustainable supplement to avoid the risk of burning these plants.",
