    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, I thought bone meal was mostly for phosphorus? My understanding was that blood meal is the heavy hitter for N-P-K nitrogen. If I use the bone meal, am I really going to see the nitrogen levels go up enough to fix the chlorosis in the kale?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While bone meal is primarily phosphorus, some high-quality bone meal products do contain a small to moderate amount of nitrogen for a slow release. Since blood meal degrades rapidly and can shock your pale kale with too much nitrogen too soon, a moderate dose of bone meal is often safer for sensitive plants than a large dose of blood meal. However, for a significant, immediate fix, blood meal is still superior because it provides much higher nitrogen right away. If cost isn't an issue, a small amount of blood meal applied carefully might work best to avoid your current pH problems.",
      "source": "target",
